<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Noe Zhordania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Noe Jordania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Noe Zhordania was a Georgian Social Democrat (Menshevik) leader who headed the government of the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from 1918 to 1921. His government held democratic elections, carried out land reform and adopted a constitution. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> invaded in 1921 he led the government in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/noe-zhordania.jpg" alt="Noe Zhordania" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E1%83%9C._%E1%83%9F%E1%83%9D%E1%83%A0%E1%83%93%E1%83%90%E1%83%9C%E1%83%98%E1%83%90.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Noe Zhordania was born on 15 January 1868 into a family of petty landowners in the village of Lanchkhuti in Guria, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, then part of the Kutais Governorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a>. His father Niko was the village scribe, a lawyer and a court mediator, and his mother, Kristine Chikovani, was Mingrelian. He studied at the Ozurgeti Orthodox Theological Academy and then at the Georgian Orthodox Theological Seminary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>. His parents hoped he would become a priest, but from an early age he disbelieved in God, and he came to regard the tsar's claim to rule by divine appointment as groundless.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the 1890s Zhordania became a leader of the first legal Marxist organisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, the Mesame Dasi, which in 1894 published a programme in the journal Moambe. Fearing arrest, he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in May 1893. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> he met Georgi Plekhanov, Leo Deutsch and Vera Zasulich, and he later studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky">Karl Kautsky</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. He wrote for the journal Kvali.</p><p>Back in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> during the revolution of 1905, he edited the clandestine newspaper Social Democrat and the legal Skhivi. He took part in the congress of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> in 1906 and was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-duma">State Duma</a>, where he led the Social Democratic faction until the Duma was dissolved after about a month.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917 he chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> soviet. On 3 September 1917 he urged workers not to yield to Bolshevik sentiment but to fight for a parliamentary republic. From 26 November 1917 he chaired the Presidium of the National Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and in May 1918 he headed the parliamentary session that declared the independent Democratic Republic of Georgia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Zhordania headed the government from 24 July 1918 until 18 March 1921, when the invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> forced him into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> he met French ambassadors, who invited him to settle the government in exile in France, but he was not warmly welcomed there in April 1921, and later appeals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> found no interest. He lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and then in Leuville-sur-Orge, where he helped prepare the Georgian revolt of 1924. He criticised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in books and founded the journals Battle and Our Flag. He led the government in exile until his death on 11 January 1953.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Noe Zhordania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Russian Revolution the Democratic Republic of Georgia declared independence on 26 May 1918 under the Menshevik <b>Noe Zhordania</b>, one of the first states in Europe to give women the vote; the Red Army entered Tbilisi on 25 February 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Governed by the Social Democratic (Menshevik) party under <b>Noe Zhordania</b>, the republic held democratic elections, carried out land reform, founded Tbilisi State University in 1918 and adopted a constitution in 1921.</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-republic-of-georgia">Democratic Republic of Georgia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lanchkhuti, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.09,42.04&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Lanchkhuti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Noe Zhordania"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5758 3934h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5758 3934h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.09,42.04&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
